 "response": "Closing-argument (60–90s): I want to be straight with you — governing means making choices that protect people today while we keep working to make things better tomorrow, and in this case the choice was between walking away and risking the loss of vital programs or accepting a compromise that closed part of a tax loophole but, most importantly, protected and increased funding for job training and apprenticeships; that vote added training slots, strengthened apprenticeship grants, and kept the employer partnerships that connect folks to real jobs in our towns, and I fought through the negotiations to defend those worker‑facing investments rather than trading them away for political points — it wasn’t a perfect bill, but I chose concrete results for working families, and I’ll keep pushing to tighten remaining loopholes and expand opportunities so people here can build careers and support their families. \n\nSocial posts: I backed a compromise that protected and grew job training and apprenticeships — that’s what I fight for; It wasn’t perfect, but I chose to save programs that lead to real jobs, not risk losing them for a slogan; This deal closed part of a loophole and kept more training slots and stronger apprenticeship grants for our community; Those are the practical wins that help people get hired by local employers — see my full town hall answer here: [link]; I’ll keep working to close remaining gaps and build more opportunities for working families."
